        "locked": false,
        "title": "exec private-LAN access fails while same user GUI/LaunchAgent succeeds",
        "body": "### Summary\n\nCodex/OpenClaw `exec` cannot reach a private-LAN host from the Mac mini, while the same macOS user and host can reach it from GUI Terminal, Browser, and a one-shot `launchd` agent.\n\nThis presents as normal OS/network failures (`No route to host`, `curl: (7)`), which makes the operator debug the LAN even though the LAN path is healthy.\n\n### Environment\n\n- Host: macOS on Mac mini, OpenClaw gateway running as user `andy`\n- Exec path: Codex app-server spawned by OpenClaw gateway\n- Target host: `192.168.147.163` (`docker-arr`)\n- Target services checked:\n  - Lidarr: `http://192.168.147.163:8686/`\n  - SSH: `docker-arr` / `192.168.147.163:22`\n\n### Reproduction\n\nFrom OpenClaw/Codex `exec` on the Mac mini:\n\n```sh\ncurl -sS -I --connect-timeout 5 http://192.168.147.163:8686/\n# curl: (7) Failed to connect to 192.168.147.163 port 8686 after ... Couldn't connect to server\n\nssh -o ConnectTimeout=5 docker-arr hostname\n# ssh: connect to host 192.168.147.163 port 22: No route to host\n```\n\nHowever, from GUI Terminal as the same user on the same Mac mini:\n\n```sh\ncurl -I http://192.168.147.163:8686/\n# HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized\n# Server: Kestrel\n\nssh -o ConnectTimeout=5 docker-arr hostname\n# docker-arr\n```\n\nA one-shot `LaunchAgent` bootstrapped into the GUI user context also succeeds:\n\n```sh\ncurl -I http://192.168.147.163:8686/\n# HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized\n# Server: Kestrel\n\nssh -o ConnectTimeout=5 docker-arr hostname\n# docker-arr\n```\n\nOne confusing detail: `traceroute -n -m 2 -w 1 192.168.147.163` from `exec` received a one-hop reply from `192.168.147.163`, while TCP/SSH/curl still failed.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIf `exec` is allowed to use host networking, it should match same-user GUI/LaunchAgent reachability.\n\nIf this traffic is intentionally blocked by an OpenClaw/Codex policy boundary, `exec` should return an explicit policy error instead of OS-looking network errors.\n\n### Actual behavior\n\n`exec` fails with network-looking errors while the same user and host can reach the target through GUI Terminal, Browser, and LaunchAgent contexts.\n\nThis led to unnecessary debugging of the switch/LAN/VPN path before discovering that the failure was specific to the OpenClaw/Codex `exec` process context.\n\n### Related but distinct issues\n\n- #76260 covers `exec` bypassing `web_fetch` SSRF blocking. This issue is the inverse failure mode: `exec` cannot reach a LAN target that the same user context can reach.\n- #39604 covers adding an opt-in private-network setting for `web_fetch`.\n- #38931 covers browser/private-network SSRF confirmation behavior.\n\nThis issue is specifically about the `exec` runtime mismatch and the misleading error surface.\n\n### Security note\n\nI am not arguing that private-LAN access should be enabled unconditionally. If the block is intended, the safer behavior would be a clear policy/approval error and consistent documentation, rather than a misleading `No route to host`.\n",
